In France, there is often hardly any need for voting: voters go into a cubicle and put a pre-printed piece of paper in an envelope that states which candidate they vote for.
Officials first check the identity of the electorate. After that, the electorate slides the envelope into the ballot box and then he puts his signature under the voting role.
The process is laid down relatively narrowly in France's electoral law.
Voters must be transparent since 1988, so that both voters and observers can see that there are no envelopes at the start of the vote and that no envelopes are added other than those of registered and authorised voters.
The candidates can send representatives to witness every part of the process. Volunteers count the votes in the evening under strict supervision and according to specific procedures.
