Thanks to the D-Day landings and the fighting that followed, the north of France was liberated. However, the south remained occupied.
It was ruled by Vichy-France. These were French who sideded with the Germans in 1940 and thus healed with the enemy instead of fighting against them.
On August 15, 1940, the Allies invaded southern France with Operation Dragoon.
The Americans and the Free French had liberated southern France within just two weeks. After that, they set out for Germany.
A civilization is a single culture that is shared by a large group of people who live and work together in a society.
From the Latin civilis, which means civic, comes the term civilization, related to the Latin civis, which in turn stands for citizen, and civitas, which entails city or city-state; and that also defines in a certain way the format of society.
City states are the forerunners of nations. A civilization with a culture suggests that knowledge over several generations is passed down, an enduring cultural footprint, and balanced spread.
When minority cultures disappear, they often leave no relevant historical evidence. Thus, they are not officially recognized as civilizations.
During the revolution, under the articles of the Confederation, thirteen states constituted a weak central government, consisting only of a congress.
The congress lacked the power to levy taxes, because there was no national executive or judiciary and for carrying out all decisions relied on state authorities that often did not want to cooperate.
Also, it did not have the power to ignore tax laws and tariffs between states.
At first, all states had to unanimously agree to the articles, before they could be adapted, but the states did not take the national government so seriously which often left their representatives absent.
