The history of Taiwan begins in the 15th century, when sailors from Europe sailed along the island and named Ilha Formosa, or beautiful island.
In 1624 the Dutch VOC established itself in the southwest of Taiwan as a base, where the VOC approached the indigenous grain production processes and put Chinese workers to work on rice and sugar plantations.
In the year 1683, the western and northern coastal regions of Taiwan were conquered by troops of the Qing Dynasty (1644-1912), after which Taiwan was annexed in the year 1885 as province of the Qing Empire.
After the defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894-1895), the Qing government in 1895 signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, in which sovereignty over Taiwan is handed over to Japan, which rules the island until 1945.
