Technological determinism is a term for a wide range of ideas in practice: from technology offerings or the technological obligation to a strict sense that human fate is driven by an underlying logic linked to scientific laws and their appearance in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism have these two general views: the development of technology follows a trajectory that for the most part extends beyond cultural or political influence, and that technology, in turn, has 'effects' on societies inherent rather thaft that are socially conditioned.
For example, some will say that the arrival of the car automatically leads to the construction of roads.
However, a national road network is not economically feasible for just a few cars. Therefore, new production methods are being developed so that the cost of car ownership can be reduced.
Massive car ownership is also causing a greater number of on-road accidents and this leads to the development of new techniques in the field of healthcare in order to be able to repair the human body.
A large part of cultural determinism was included in romance, which came from writers such as Goethe, Fichte and Schlegel.
In Romantics, geography formed the individual and over time habits and cultures arose in response to this geography. These constituted a harmonious society and were better than the arbitrary imposed laws.
Constantinople was seen as the fashion capital of feudal Europe, as Paris is today's fashion capital.
It was known as an epicenter of luxury from about 400 AD to about 1100 AD.
The status of the empire decreased in the 12th century, mainly because the crusaders came back with silk and spices that were worth more than what the Byzantine markets could offer.
Around this time, Paris also took over the title of fashion capital from Constantinople.
The Gothic celebrated its heyday in the 10th, 11th and 14th centuries.
In the beginning, clothes were strongly influenced by Byzantine culture from the east.
However, due to the slow communication channels, styles in the west were 25 to 30 years behind.
At the end of the Middle Ages, a style of their own began in Western Europe. One of the biggest developments was the result of the Crusades: people began to use knots to attach their clothes.
Self-sufficient agriculture is agriculture where sufficient food is produced to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Agriculture with the aim of living is a simple system that is usually organic. Stored seed that originates from the ecoregion is used, and that is combined with changing crops or other relatively simple techniques to optimize the yield.
In the past, most farmers worked in self-sufficient agriculture. This is still the case in many developing countries.
Subcultures ensure that like-minded people who feel neglected are brought together by social norms. This gives them the opportunity to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures can distinguish themselves through the age, ethnicity, social class, location and/or gender of their members.
A subculture can be determined by the following types of qualities: linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical or one or more of these factors together.
Members of a subculture often show which group they belong to through a certain symbolic style in what they wear, in how they behave and the jargon they speak.
One of the most commonly used methods for illustrating the importance of socialization were the few deplorable cases of children not socialized by adults due to negligence, setbacks or wilful abuse while growing up.
They are called 'twelve children' or 'wild children'. Some wolf children have been imprisoned by people (usually their own parents); sometimes this child was abandoned, because the parents did not want to accept the severe mental or physical disability of the child.
Unruly children may have experienced serious child abuse or severe trauma before they were abandoned or before they walked away.
Others are claimed to have been raised by animals, and some are said to have lived alone in the wilderness.
A feral child who has not been raised by humans may exhibit behaviors similar to the behavior of the animal that has cared for the child (within the physical limits), such as fear or indifference towards people.
Learning on a project basis should simplify education and make it more interesting, but scaffolding goes a step further.
Scaffolding is not a learning method but a tool to support individuals who are dealing with a new learning experience, such as a new computer program or starting a new project.
Support can be both virtual and real. For example, a teacher is a form of support, but Microsoft Office’s paperclip guy is too.
There is virtual scaffolding that is built into the software that asks questions, provides reminders, and explains procedures that can potentially be difficult to understand for students without assistance.
