Research in the twentieth century has shown that there are two groups of genetic variants: hidden and visible.
New genetic variation is added by mutation; by selection this variation is eliminated from the pole of expressed variation.
For each generation there is a variety between the two groups by means of separation and recombination.
A primate with a digestive system similar to that of humans can only hardly get the necessary amino acids from the plant sources that can be found there.
Furthermore, it cannot take into account serious consequences: growth delay, malnutrition and ultimately even death.
The easiest-to-find plant sources are the proteins in leaves and legumes, but unless they are cooked, they are difficult to digest for primates like us.
Not only is animal food (ants, termites, eggs) easily digestible, but it also contains a lot of proteins that contain all essential amino acids.
All in all, we shouldn't be surprised if our ancestors solved their 'protein problem' in the same way as chimpanzees on the savannah solve this problem today.
Sleep interruption means that you wake up for a reason during your normal sleep period and that you fall back asleep within a short time (10–60 minutes).
This is quite easy by using a relatively soft alarm clock that brings you back to your consciousness without immediately waking up completely.
If you turn off your clock in your sleep, you can place it on the other side of the room. This way, you need to get out of bed to turn it off.
