Stirrings support the rider's feet and hang down on both sides of the saddle.
They provide the rider with more stability, but can also entail problems with safety because the feet of a rider can get stuck in it.
If a rider is thrown off a horse but lingers with the foot in the stirrup, they can be dragged along with the horse. To reduce the risk, a number of safety measures have been taken.
Most riders first wear riding boots with a heel and a smooth, fairly narrow sole.
Some saddles, and especially English saddles, have safety brackets. This allows a stirrup leather from the saddle to come loose if this is pulled backwards because a rider falls off the horse.
