The Japanese working environment is more structured and formal in nature than in the Western world.
Suits are standard work clothes and colleagues call each other at the surname or job name.
Unanimity in the workplace is critical. The focus is on group performance rather than pricing individual work performance.
Employees often have to get permission from their executives before they can make a decision and they are expected to follow the executives' instructions thoughtlessly.
